WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.760
I think cars should come with
health warnings like cigarettes.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:08.840
Why?

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.000
Consider this… 

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.040
What cigarette do you smoke doctor?

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.320
It might sound incredible now,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.960
but there was a time when
doctors appeared in adverts

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.160
encouraging us to smoke.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.640
You could smoke in restaurants
and pubs, buses and planes. 

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.240
Now, cigarette packets come
with stark and graphic warnings

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.920
about the impact of smoking on your
health - and on those around you. 

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.960
In the future, will we look back
on this era - the rise of the car -

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.800
with similar incredulity? 

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.040
I think so. 

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.240
During the height of the
coronavirus lockdown,

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.320
daily emissions of carbon dioxide
fell by 17% around the world. 

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:54.200
Seventeen million tonnes less
carbon dioxide was emitted every day,

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:58.120
with almost half of that drop
because of fewer car journeys.  

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.200
Air pollution in some UK cities
dropped by as much as 60%. 

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.520
There are more than a billion
cars around the world.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.920
And as we reassess
and restructure our world,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.560
I think we need to reassess
our relationship -

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.280
you could call it a love affair -
with the car. 

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.320
What’s interesting about our response

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.440
to the threats from both
smoking and the pandemic,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.920
is how we acted and
changed our behaviour

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.400
to help protect each other’s health.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.560
We don’t blow cigarette smoke
in each other’s faces anymore.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.040
Or light up inside.

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.960
With the coronavirus, we quickly
learned to give each other space,

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.160
wear masks, wash our hands -

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.440
and we got used to all the signs
reminding us to do so.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.920
It’s normal when
there’s a threat to life

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.760
for us to change our behaviour
and to do whatever it takes

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.080
to protect friends,
family and other people.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.920
So why do we put up with
the air pollution pandemic,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.400
when there’s something
that we can do about it?

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:06.800
Why don’t we have reminders on cars

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.760
about the consequences
of street level pollution,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.000
and encourage us
to walk or cycle more? 

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.840
Still not convinced? 

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.760
Here are a few things you need to
be aware of when it comes to cars.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.840
Studies have shown air pollution -
much of which comes from cars -

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.760
contributes to the
premature deaths

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.360
of almost half a million people
in Europe every year. 

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.960
The impact on our bodies
is truly frightening. 

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.960
Air pollution can trigger heart
attacks, strokes, and asthma,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.800
and contribute to stunted growth,
low birth weight, and lung cancer. 

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.600
Car tyre wear also contributes
hugely to air pollution,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.320
but it’s a largely unregulated area. 

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.880
As you drive on the road,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.480
your car tyres shed tiny bits
of tyre material - microplastics -

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.480
which get washed into our drains,

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:00.360
and ultimately
our rivers and oceans.  

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.400
This happens regardless of whether
your car is diesel, petrol,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.240
hybrid, or electric.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.040
And there’s another
big problem with cars -

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.080
and that’s their impact
on climate change. 

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.000
You only have to drive 500 metres
in an average modern car

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.000
to melt at least
1 kilo of glacier ice.  

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.080
Since 2010, SUVs have been
the second biggest source

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.160
of rising carbon emissions. 

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.640
If SUV drivers were a nation,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.320
they’d be the world’s 7th biggest
carbon polluter. 

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.080
Every time you use your car,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.280
you’re contributing to the
degradation of the planet.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.960
A lot of this is just unnecessary. 

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.560
Here in the UK,

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.880
even when trips are only between
one and two miles long - 3km -

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.800
six out of 10 people still drive. 

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.240
In circumstances no-one would
ever have wished for,

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.320
the coronavirus pandemic
gave us a small glimpse

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.480
of what a different - cleaner,
greener, less polluted, world -

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.760
could be like. 

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.920
Putting health warnings on cars

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.480
could be a stark and clear
visual reminder to all of us

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.600
about the devastating impact they
have on both people and the planet.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.400
And help shift our attitude
to cars once and for all.

